-- 0319 — THE HARBOURS HAVE THEIR ISLANDS  (owner row 92; row 41, the fleet never touches land)
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- GENERATED by scripts/build-sea-migration.mjs with ISLANDS_CLOSE_WATER. Do not hand-edit: the raster is
-- scripts/sea-grid.mjs over data/world-110m.json, the mend is scripts/lib/sea-islands-emit.mjs.
-- File name: 20260818000319_the_harbours_have_their_islands.sql. THE NUMBER IS SET AT DEPLOY (prod head + 1, in order).
--
-- THE OWNER: "some cities are in the ocean, not on land such as istanbul. I want you to fix it." and,
-- 2026-11-05, "some cities are in ocean, they are meant to be like that yes?"
--
-- WHAT MOVES
--   1. public.sea_raster: 182 cells closed, 0 opened, 545,790 water cells left. The 28 islands the 110m sheet
--      lacked are in the land file now, and each closes every cell its drawn ring touches.
--   2. public.sea_reaches: 28 roadsteads move off their quays (below), and the 12,359 of 33,670 pairs whose sailed
--      distance the islands actually changed take the new figure. Both sides are measured by TODAY's
--      pathfinder, old water against new, so a pair the islands did not touch keeps its figure to the tenth.
--      Over the changed pairs: mean 7.83 nm, widest +169.6 / -257.8 nm. 0 pairs unreachable.
--   3. Every stored course that ends at a moved roadstead or crosses a closed cell is MENDED, here, at
--      apply time: public.standing_route_stops, public.npc_courses and any pending SAIL order. Its end goes
--      to the new roadstead and the stretch that crossed the island goes round it, cell by cell, then
--      straightened. The rest of the course is left point for point.
--   4. What is derived from the above: public.sea_leg_asks and port_goods.haul / haul_nm (world.haul_rows,
--      which the world guard holds equal). A longer haul lifts a price a little; a shorter one lowers it.
--   5. public.sea_undo_0319 keeps the pre-image of all of it, for the forward rollback
--      (docs/dev-log/2026-11-05-islands-on-the-chart.md). Server-only.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH: public.sea_cells (a closed cell keeps its sea name, like ICE: most of the cell is
-- still water, nobody sails it), public.sea_zone_cells, voyage.channels, any function body, any knob.
-- A voyage already at sea keeps her frozen course and docks on her ETA: she may cross an island once.
